==English==

===Etymology===
From {{prefix|en|in|glorious}}; from {{uder|en|la|inglōrius}}; first known use: 1565-75.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈɡlɔɹi.əs/|/ɪnˈɡloʊɹi.əs/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|in|glo|ri|ous}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔːɹiəs|s=4}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[ignominious|Ignominious]]; [[disgraceful]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King John|act=V|scene=i|passage=O '''inglorious''' league! / Shall we, upon the footing of our land, / Send fair-play orders and make compromise, / Insinuation, parley and base truce / To arms invasive?}}
#* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Frankenstein|chapter=6|passage=Resolved to pursue no '''inglorious''' career, he turned his eyes toward the East.}}
#* {{RQ:London White Fang|part=3|chapter=6|passage=He cast about in his mind for a way to beat a retreat not too '''inglorious'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1945|month=September and October|author=C. Hamilton Ellis|title=Royal Trains—V|journal=Railway Magazine|page=251|text=The last occasion on which the Kaiser [Wilhelm II] used this train was for an '''inglorious''' journey into Holland towards the end of the 1914 war. He spent the night in it at Eysden [{{l|en|Eijsden}}], while the Queen of the Netherlands and a hastily summoned Cabinet debated what to do with him.}}
# Not [[famous]]; [[obscure]].

